<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Daugavpils</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Dvinsk, Dünaburg</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Daugavpils is the second largest city of Latvia, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-dvina">Daugava</a> river in the south-eastern region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latgale">Latgale</a>. Founded by the Livonian Order as Dünaburg in the 13th century, it later belonged to Poland-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a>, as Dvinsk. Latvian and Polish forces took it from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> in January 1920, and it has a large Russian-speaking population.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/daugavpils.jpg" alt="Daugavpils" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ivo Kruusamägi · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Daugavpils_luter%C4%81%C5%86u_bazn%C4%ABca_01.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
